 {"status": "complete", "id": "AUG-125", "title": "Published XSD cannot be loaded by any XSD 1.0 processor", "detail": {"fingerprint": "21ae180fded0e074e12af5802d4307ad9f6f601dbace4ccd3df5181ae68fa037", "evidence": [{"run_id": "5c0158a1", "ts": "2026-11-10T03:09:29.056495+00:00", "tree_fp": "7a62f8dfed3ded4c957da54059f4ed459793642229296d578436d221e4337b4e"}]}, "priority": "P1", "source": null, "reasoning": "Carter review 2026-11-03 @6c8f1cc. specs/schema/auger-spec-1.0.xsd uses Python-style non-capturing groups in two xs:pattern facets (line 28 '^[A-Z][A-Z0-9]*(?:-[A-Z0-9]+)+$' and line 43 '^[a-z0-9]+(?:[._-][a-z0-9]+)*$'). XML Schema 1.0 regex has no (?: construct, so libxml2/lxml refuses the schema itself: XMLSchemaParseError: value ... of the facet 'pattern' is not a valid regular expression, line 28. Replacing (?: with ( makes it load, so the cause is confirmed by experiment, not inferred. docs/SPEC-003 calls this file the normative grammar, yet no editor or consumer can validate against it. tests/test_xml_specs.py::test_schema_matches_the_validator only compares enumerations and pattern STRINGS, so it cannot notice that both sides use a dialect XSD does not have. Evidence: /home/kara/project-reviews/reviews/auger/evidence/2026-11-03/ (report 2026-11-03-carter.html F1).", "review_notes": null, "ts": null, "acceptance_criteria": "PASS: a conforming XSD 1.0 processor loads specs/schema/auger-spec-1.0.xsd without error (prove with lxml.etree.XMLSchema or xmllint --schema, both named in the test). PASS: every shipped fixture under tests/fixtures/xml/ validates against the shipped XSD in the same test. PASS: the parity test fails if a pattern is changed to a construct the processor rejects (mutation check), so 'schema matches code' means dialect-valid too. PASS: all xs:pattern facets still express the same language as the Python validator for a sample of accepted and rejected IDs.", "complexity": 3, "created_at": "2026-11-03T19:58:25.923322+00:00", "updated_at": "202